<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Joseph Stalin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1878">1878</a>–<a href="/years/1953">1953</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Iosif Vissarionovich Stalin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Joseph Stalin (1878–1953) was the leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> from the late 1920s until his death. A Georgian Bolshevik, he rose through the post of general secretary of the Communist Party after Lenin's death and imposed forced collectivisation, rapid industrialisation and mass terror that cost millions of lives, including the famine of 1932–1933. He led the Soviet Union to victory over Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and extended its control over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin.jpg" alt="Joseph Stalin" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Franklin D. Roosevelt Library Public Domain Photographs · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:StalinCropped1943.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Stalin was born Ioseb Besarionis dze Jughashvili on 18 December 1878 (6 December in the Old Style calendar) in Gori, in the Tiflis Governorate of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>. He was the third child of Besarion Jughashvili and Ekaterine Geladze, and the only one to survive infancy. His father's shoemaking workshop declined, the family fell into poverty, and Besarion became an alcoholic who beat his wife and son. In 1894 Stalin enrolled with a scholarship at the Tiflis Theological Seminary as a trainee <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> priest, but he lost interest, read <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Marx</a> and attended secret workers' meetings. He left in April 1899. In November 1901 he was elected to the Tiflis Committee of the Russian Social Democratic Labour Party.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a> of 1917 Stalin joined Lenin's inner circle and became People's Commissar for Nationalities. After Lenin's death in January 1924 a struggle for the succession followed, in which Stalin used his post as General Secretary to place loyalists across the party and, with Kamenev and Zinoviev, formed a triumvirate against Trotsky.</p><p>From 1928 his five-year plans brought rapid collectivisation and industrialisation. State policy contributed to a major famine in the early 1930s, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a> most affected. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-purge">Great Purge</a> of 1936–1939 at least 700,000 people were executed and over a million imprisoned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulag">Gulag</a>. In August 1939 his government signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molotov-ribbentrop-pact">Molotov–Ribbentrop Pact</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, with a secret protocol dividing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>. Germany invaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in 1941, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> in 1945.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the war the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulag">Gulag</a> system was expanded further, and by January 1953 three percent of the Soviet population was imprisoned or in internal exile. Stalin died on 5 March 1953 and was succeeded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikita-khrushchev">Nikita Khrushchev</a>, who denounced him and began <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/de-stalinization">de-Stalinisation</a>.</p><p>Opinion of him remains divided. Most Westerners and anti-communist Russians view him as a mass murderer, significant numbers of Russians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgians">Georgians</a> regard him as a great statesman, and his regime has been widely condemned for mass repression, ethnic cleansing and the deaths of millions.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Joseph Stalin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The khanate is the historical homeland state of the Crimean Tatars, whose national identity, language and memory of statehood remain tied to it, especially after their mass deportation by <b>Stalin</b>’s government in 1944 and their return from the late 1980s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crimean-khanate">Crimean Khanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>North Vietnam was a unitary and centralised Marxist–Leninist state with a constitution modelled on <b>Joseph Stalin</b>'s 1936 Constitution of the Soviet Union; its first constitution was adopted by the National Assembly in November 1946.</blockquote><a href="/empire/democratic-republic-of-vietnam">Democratic Republic of Vietnam · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1928 <b>Joseph Stalin</b>’s five-year plans transformed the republic, which held most of the Soviet Union’s industry and resources: new industrial centres such as Magnitogorsk rose in the Urals and Siberia, cities grew rapidly and literacy became almost universal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-sfsr">Russian SFSR · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>To regulate relations between these nominally sovereign republics, the Bolshevik leaders debated their form: <b>Joseph Stalin</b>, People's Commissar for Nationalities, proposed absorbing them into the Russian republic as autonomous units, while Lenin insisted on a federation of formally equal republics.</blockquote><a href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Bolsheviks seized power in Russia in 1917 and created the Soviet Union, where <b>Joseph Stalin</b> had established personal and totalitarian rule by 1929; Benito Mussolini came to power in Italy in 1922 and from 1925 built the first fascist dictatorship; and Adolf Hitler, appointed chancellor in January…</blockquote><a href="/government/dictatorship">Dictatorship · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>Lenin and his successors argued that the working class needed a single, disciplined vanguard to take power and then to defend it: “the proletariat needs the Party first of all as its General Staff”, <b>Joseph Stalin</b> wrote in The Foundations of Leninism (1924).</blockquote><a href="/government/one-party-state">One-party state · Strengths</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Gori, in what is now <a href="/countries/georgia">Georgia</a>. Died in Kuntsevo Dacha, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.98,44.11&amp;year=1926" aria-label="Gori on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Joseph Stalin"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5888 3928h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5888 3928h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.98,44.11&amp;year=1926">What was here in 1926? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
